---
.title = "Hexadecimal Encoding and Decoding",
.date = "2026-10-01",
.author = "ZigCC",
.layout = "section.shtml",
---

Hexadecimal (hex) encoding is extensively used for representing binary digests, cryptographic keys, and debugging raw packet structures. In Zig, the standard library offers zero-allocation primitives for both encoding and decoding.

Key features:

- `std.fmt.bytesToHex(bytes, .lower | .upper)`: Encodes a byte array into a fixed-size `[bytes.len * 2]u8` value without dynamic heap allocation, supporting both lowercase and uppercase formats;
- `std.fmt.hexToBytes(out_buf, hex_str)`: Decodes a hexadecimal string directly into a caller-provided byte buffer;
- Error handling: Returns `error.InvalidCharacter` upon encountering non-hexadecimal characters, and `error.InvalidLength` when the input string length is odd.

